#
# Local url:
URL='http://localhost:9000'

# GitHub:
# If you'd like to see live GitHub data in /members or the sponsors section, you'll need a token from github
# https://docs.github.com/en/github/authenticating-to-github/creating-a-personal-access-token
# The token does not need any permissions, so you can leave all of the "scope" boxes unchecked.
# One you have your token, uncomment the following line and add your token
# GITHUB_TOKEN=yourtoken
#
# Google Calendar (events):
# The /events page and the homepage event list read a Google Calendar through a service account.
# If you'd like to see live event data, ask a maintainer for the service account key JSON and the
# calendar id. Paste the key JSON on a single line inside single quotes. Both values are required;
# without them the site uses mock event data.
#
# GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY='{"type":"service_account","project_id":"...","private_key":"...","client_email":"..."}'
# GOOGLE_CALENDAR_ID=xxxx@group.calendar.google.com
#
# /admin/events edits that calendar. Outside production every write is captured (logged, reported
# as done, nothing sent). To try the writes for real, create a scratch calendar, share it with the
# service account's email as "Make changes to events", put its id in GOOGLE_CALENDAR_ID, and set:
#
# CALENDAR_LIVE_OUTSIDE_PRODUCTION=
#
# Redirect handlers (/join-coffee in netlify/functions/, /join-slack in src/app/join-slack/):
#
# Only needed if you're working on those functions locally — production values live in
# Netlify's env settings.
#
# SLACK_JOIN_LINK=https://join.slack.com/t/...
# ZOOM_TUESDAYS=https://zoom.us/j/...
# ZOOM_THURSDAYS=https://zoom.us/j/...
#
# Bot blocking (/netlify/edge-functions/block-bots.ts):
#
# The blocklist is skipped in local dev. Uncomment to exercise it against
# localhost — deployed environments always enforce it regardless of this value.
#
# BLOCK_BOTS_LOCAL=true
#
# Sentry (error monitoring):
#
# Off without a DSN. Netlify sets this for every deploy context; set it
# locally only when you want your own errors reported (they are tagged
# environment=development). Maintainers: the DSN is in the Sentry project
# settings. SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN (source-map upload) is a Netlify build secret
# and is never needed locally.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=https://publickey@o0.ingest.us.sentry.io/0
##
# ── Membership database and /admin ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The database itself needs no configuration: `netlify dev` starts a local
# Postgres and @netlify/database finds it. Run `pnpm db:migrate` once, then
# `pnpm db:seed` for realistic sample applications.
#
# Without Slack credentials, run `pnpm db:seed` and pick a test user from the
# "Auth DevTools" panel on /admin/sign-in (or /invites/sign-in). Local only:
# deploy previews sign in with real Slack OAuth through production's callback
# (docs/adr/0007).
#
# Slack OAuth (maintainers only). The client id and secret are the vc-bots
# Slack app's; SLACK_TEAM_ID pins sign-in to the Virtual Coffee workspace.
# Sign-in from here goes through production's registered redirect URI, the
# same way a deploy preview's does, so OAUTH_PROXY_SECRET must be production's
# value — ask a maintainer. No localhost redirect URI is needed.
#
# SLACK_CLIENT_ID=
# SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET=
# SLACK_TEAM_ID=T00000000
# OAUTH_PROXY_SECRET=
#
# Better Auth. The secret signs sessions; generate one with
# `openssl rand -base64 32`. Its base URL is `URL` above.
#
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
#
# Comma-separated Slack member ids that become admins the first time they sign
# in. This only bootstraps the first admin: after that, roles are granted and
# revoked in /admin, and someone who has not signed in yet gets a Pending Grant
# from the "Grant access" picker instead. Removing an id here revokes nothing.
#
# Find yours in Slack: your profile, then the ⋮ menu, "Copy member ID".
#
# ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_SLACK_IDS=U0123ABCDEF
#
# Transactional email, sent through Google Workspace from hello@virtualcoffee.io.
# The key is a Google Cloud service account's JSON key file, pasted whole; the
# account has domain-wide delegation with the https://mail.google.com/ scope.
# Not the calendar's GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY. hello@ is a group, so the
# account signs in as GOOGLE_SMTP_USER, a real user who must have hello@ as a
# Gmail "Send mail as" alias — without it Gmail rewrites the From to that user.
#
# Only production (Netlify CONTEXT=production) delivers to recipients. Locally
# and on previews every send is *captured*: written to the function log,
# reported as sent, never delivered — so these two are never needed here. See
# docs/adr/0013.
#
# GOOGLE_SMTP_USER=joe@virtualcoffee.io
# GMAIL_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY='{"type":"service_account","client_id":"...","private_key":"-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...","client_email":"..."}'
#
# Or point local dev at a locally running SMTP sink instead — e.g. Mailpit
# (https://mailpit.axllent.org/, SMTP on :1025, web UI on :8025). No
# credentials needed: GOOGLE_SMTP_USER/GMAIL_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY are not read
# when this is set. Mail is addressed exactly as production would address it
# — nothing leaves the machine. Only a checkout honours it; a deploy ignores
# it. Only localhost, 127.0.0.1 and ::1 count, so a checkout cannot relay real
# mail through a remote SMTP host; for Mailpit in Docker, publish its port and
# use localhost, not the container name. See docs/adr/0013.
#
# SMTP_HOST=localhost
# SMTP_PORT=1025
#
# Slack posts and GitHub issues are captured the same way. Set this to `true`
# to let them go out from a non-production deploy — and point the webhooks
# below at a test channel first. It does not cover the DM sent to a member
# when access is granted: that is captured outside production, full stop.
#
# NOTIFY_LIVE_OUTSIDE_PRODUCTION=
#
# Slack notifications for the four public forms. These replace the Airtable
# automations that used to post them. One incoming webhook per destination, so
# a missing one only silences its own form — create them on the vc-bots Slack
# app. Without them a submission is still saved, and the failure is recorded
# and shown on the /admin dashboard.
#
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_COC=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_VOLUNTEERS=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_LUNCH_AND_LEARN=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_COFFEE_TABLES=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...
#
# The membership pipeline's own channel: every new application is announced
# there, and one that claims a volunteer invite is flagged because it jumps to
# the front of the waitlist:
#
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_MEMBERSHIP=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...
#
# Bot token with the `users:read` and `users:read.email` scopes, for the Slack
# member directory behind the "Grant access" picker in /admin/user-management
# and the "Add a volunteer" picker in /admin/volunteers (which prefills the
# email); add `im:write` and `chat:write` on top for DMing someone when access
# is granted or a "Resend DM" is clicked in /admin/user-management
# (conversations.open then chat.postMessage). A different credential from
# SLACK_CLIENT_* above: sign-in only asks for OIDC scopes, which cannot call
# any of those. Without it the pickers fall back to mock members and a grant's
# DM is reported as failed rather than sent.
#
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-...
#
# The GitHub App, for opening the Lunch & Learn issue in
# Virtual-Coffee/VC-Community-Docs. The App must be installed on that repo with
# the issues permission.
#
# Same App and same values as the CI_APP_* Actions secrets the workflows use —
# only the variable names differ between the two environments. This is not
# GITHUB_TOKEN above, which stays a permission-less read-only PAT.
#
# GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
